## 5. DTO, entity, mapper — constraints and side effects

### Request DTO — `OwnerFieldsDto` (OpenAPI-generated)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/target/generated-sources/openapi/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/dto/OwnerFieldsDto.java`

Per-field bean-validation constraints (getter annotations):
- `firstName`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `lastName`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[\\p{L}]+([ '-][\\p{L}]+){0,2}\\.?$") @Size(min = 1, max = 30)`
- `address`: `@NotNull @Size(min = 1, max = 255)`
- `city`: `@NotNull @Size(min = 1, max = 80)`
- `telephone`: `@NotNull @Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]*$") @Size(min = 1, max = 20)`

Important: because step 1 binds the body **without `@Valid`**, these DTO constraints are effectively dormant on this endpoint. For example, a non-blank but pattern-violating `firstName` (e.g. digits) or a `telephone` of 15 digits (violates DTO `@Size(max=20)`? no — 15 is within 20) is NOT rejected by the DTO layer. Only the entity constraints (below) can still catch some of these at save time.

The DTO fields, plain setters/getters, `equals`/`hashCode`/`toString` are as generated; no lifecycle side effects.

### Entity — `Owner`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Owner.java`

Constraints on entity fields:
- `address`: `@NotEmpty`
- `city`: `@NotEmpty`
- `telephone`: `@NotEmpty @Digits(fraction = 0, integer = 10) @Pattern(regexp = "^[0-9]{10}$", message = "Phone number must be exactly 10 digits")`
- `pets`: `@OneToMany(cascade = CascadeType.ALL, mappedBy = "owner", fetch = FetchType.EAGER)`

Notable entity-level side effects / behavior:
- `telephone` is far stricter than the DTO: exactly 10 digits (`@Digits(integer=10)` + `@Pattern ^[0-9]{10}$`). A valid-by-DTO phone (1–20 digits) can still fail entity validation at persistence. This is a divergence worth flagging.
- `addPet(Pet)` mutates `getPetsInternal()` and back-references `pet.setOwner(this)` — but is not invoked in the create-owner pipeline (mapper ignores `pets`).
- `setPets` replaces the internal set; `getPets` returns an unmodifiable name-sorted copy.

### Base class — `Person` (firstName/lastName)
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/model/Person.java`

- `firstName`: `@NotEmpty`
- `lastName`: `@NotEmpty`
- `@MappedSuperclass`; plain getters/setters, no side effects.

### Mapper — `OwnerMapper`
`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`

Relevant mapping used by `BuildOwner`:

```java
@Mapping(target = "id", ignore = true)
@Mapping(target = "pets", ignore = true)
Owner toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto ownerDto);
```

So on create, `id` and `pets` are never copied from the request; the new `Owner` has a null id (DB-generated on save) and no pets. `toOwnerDto(Owner)` is used by the responder.

